[Music]  [Applause]  welcome back to cbs mornings we now turn  to margaret brennan's exclusive  wide-ranging cbs news interview with  vice president kamala harris the vice  president spoke with margaret one day  after senator joe manchin said he  couldn't vote for president president  biden's build back better plan a major  setback for the president's domestic  agenda listen to what the vice president  said about that  do you feel that senator manchin is  playing fair with you i mean he went on  television and said no pretty  definitively i think the stakes are too  high for this to be in any way about any  specific individual we have to you know  one of the things the 50 50 senate  though so it is  the stakes are so high  i refuse to get caught up in the what  might be personal politics  when the people who are  waking up at three o'clock in the  morning worried about how they're going  to get by  could care less about the politics of  d.c so you don't feel betrayed  no i don't feel i don't have any  personal feelings about this this is  about let's get the job done and talk  with families who say i can't afford  to do the basic things that i need to do  as a responsible adult like care for my  children care for my older parents or  afford to get life-saving medication  like insulin we have to do that without  center mansion you don't give up that's  how we do it  margaret brennan who's moderator of face  the nation ncbs news chief foreign  affairs correspondent joins us right now  good morning margaret now the vice  president says they are not giving up on  the president's agenda um do they need a  back-up plan to the buildback better  plan  or rebuild build back better as senator  joe manchin said nate yes well the vice  president in our interview was very  clearly trying to lower the temperature  because the reality of the situation is  that they cannot afford to be angry for  very long unless the white house can  peel off the support of  some republicans they need senator joe  manchin they need his vote to get  virtually anything done in a 50 50  evenly divided senate they need all  democrats and it is not a good look  after eight months of infighting for  this to be front page news that they're  having problems wrangling their own  party so they need senator manchin to  change the tax laws immigration climate  and voting rights and that is another  topic that i spoke with the vice  president about at length she feels very  passionately  about trying to pass two voting rights  laws at the federal level because of  what's happening in at least seven or 18  states where they're changing um how to  cast votes so she emphasized that as  really the biggest threat to our country  on the national security front nate and  i thought that was very interesting at  the integrity of our democracy at itself  so i suspect you will hear about  potential changes to how the senate  votes and and their rules of engagement  well she's certainly being the grown-up  in the room and saying this isn't  personal because i hear a lot of  democrats when it comes to joe manchin  they're very er very angry and i hear a  lot of why  why i ought with him so it's very  interesting that they're taking that  tactic it's probably the best thing to  do at this point i know you must have  talked to her about omicron and what the  white house how they feel about it how  they're dealing with it are they ready  for it because it is a mess  it is scale and and we know that it is  the virus that determines the path of  the economy it may also determine the  political fate of this white house so uh  on camera and the the vice president was  very clear that they believe they are  prepared for this  but and our health system is prepared  but as you know the president is going  to be making this address to the public  today to try to do a little bit more  where whether it's at home testing or to  try to reassure the public because they  know they cannot afford economically or  politically to go into another lockdown  and that is another topic we talked  about you know on the back end of this  looking at the economy  will they need another rescue package  and that brings you back again to the  need to have some full support in  congress  because  right now  price inflation is going to continue  yeah margaret looking overseas there is  the possibility of a war between russia  and ukraine and there's a question mark  about how involved the u.s would become  if that happened you asked the vice  president about all of that let's listen  and then talk about her response  there are a hundred thousand russian  troops on the border with ukraine  are we going to see a hot war in europe  in the next few weeks  well we are  having direct conversations with russia  with the president as you know met  recently virtually with  putin  and  we are very clear  that  that russia should not  invade  the sovereignty of ukraine that we must  stand up and we are standing up for its  territorial integrity  we are working with our allies in that  regard and we've been very clear that we  are prepared to issue sanctions like  you've not seen before does that mean  sanctioning vladimir putin directly i'm  not going to talk about specific  sanctions but we are making that clear  to him and we are in direct  conversations  interesting margaret what'd you think of  that response  well financial sanctions have not  stopped vladimir putin before so this  seems to be a very direct threat in a  different nature from this white house  but tony i continue to hear from u.s  officials that they are very concerned  that vladimir putin will take some  action whether it's a destabilized  destabilizing cyber attack  or it's actually moving those 100 000  troops across the border so we are not  out of the woods the white house again  made an offer to continue uh diplomacy  with moscow the vice president is never  short of words that's for sure margaret  thank you

